NNAAMMEE
       ppmcolormask - produce mask of areas of a certain color in a PPM file


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk --ccoolloorr==_c_o_l_o_r___l_i_s_t [_p_p_m_f_i_l_e]

       ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk _c_o_l_o_r [_p_p_m_f_i_l_e]


EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
           ppmcolormask -color red testimg.ppm >redmask.pbm
           pamcomp background.ppm testimg.ppm -alpha=redmask.pbm >test.ppm

           ppmcolormask -color=red,pink,salmon testimg.ppm >reddishmask.pbm

           ppmcolormask -color=bk:red,bk:orange,bk:yellow testimg.ppm >firemask.pbm




DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk reads a PPM file as input and produces a PBM (bitmap) file
       as output.  The output file is the same dimensions as  the  input  file
       and is black in all places where the input file is a color indicated by
       the --ccoolloorr option, and white everywhere else.

       The output of ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk is useful as a transparency  mask  input  to
       ppaammccoommpp.   Note that you don't need ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk and ppaammccoommpp if you are
       ultimately converting to PNG with ppnnmmttooppnngg because the --ttrraannssppaarreenntt op‐
       tion on ppnnmmttooppnngg does the same thing.

       _p_p_m_f_i_l_e  is the input file.  If you don't specify _p_p_m_f_i_l_e, the input is
       from Standard Input.

       The output goes to Standard Output.

       In the obsolete alternative syntax, specifying the _c_o_l_o_r names a single
       exact color to be masked.

       ppppmmcchhaannggee does a similar thing: it modifies an image by changing colors
       you specify to other colors you specify.  The  two  programs  give  you
       somewhat different means of specifying colors in the input image.

       To make a mask of an image's background, without having to tell it what
       color it is, use ppaammbbaacckkggrroouunndd.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk recognizes
       the following command line option:



       --ccoolloorr==_c_o_l_o_r___l_i_s_t
              This mandatory option specifies the colors that are to be masked
              (where the image is one of these colors, the output mask will be
              black).

              Examples:



       •      --ccoolloorr==rreedd

       •      --ccoolloorr==rreedd,,ppiinnkk,,ssaallmmoonn

       •      --ccoolloorr==rrggbb::8800//8800//ffff

       •      --ccoolloorr==bbkk::rreedd,,bbkk::oorraannggee,,bbkk::yyeellllooww


              _c_o_l_o_r___l_i_s_t is a list of colors separated by commas.  Each  color
              is  either  an exact color name as described for the argument of
              the          ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())          library           routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩  or one of the Berlin-Kay color
              names ⟨libppm.html#berlinkay⟩ .  In the latter case, all  colors
              that are better described by that Berlin-Kay color name than any
              other are in the mask set.

              The algorithm ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk uses to determine to which  colors  a
              Berlin-Kay  color  name  applies is based on a Sugeno-type fuzzy
              inference system developed by  _K_e_n_a_n  _K_a_l_a_j_d_z_i_c  in  2006.   The
              fuzzy  model  consists  of partially linear membership functions
              defined in the HSV color space.   Although  more  complex  algo‐
              rithms  for fuzzy color matching exist, this algorithm is inten‐
              tionally simplified to achieve a satisfactory speed using  rela‐
              tively compact code.

              This  option  was new in Netpbm 10.34 (June 2006).  Before that,
              you must use the _c_o_l_o_r argument and cannot specify a  Berlin-Kay
              color.




